The heuristic returns the parent of the first `file_list('.')` entry containing a `/`. Two edge cases would silently produce a wrong root and re-create the exact "closure silently empty" failure this change fixes:

- If `file_list` ever returns directory entries with a trailing slash (e.g. `js/`), `lastIndexOf('/')` hits that trailing slash and `ROOT` becomes `js` — a relative, wrong root.
- The `'.'` fallback at the end swallows "no usable entry found" instead of surfacing it.

Since the surrounding comment explicitly calls out the silently-empty closure as the incident class, consider a cheap sanity probe so a bad root fails the test immediately rather than passing vacuously:

